<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dimasa people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dimasa Kacharis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dimasa, also called Dimasa Kacharis, are a Tibeto-Burman people of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dima-hasao">Dima Hasao</a>, Karbi Anglong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a> districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagaland">Nagaland</a>. They speak Dimasa, a Boro-Garo language, and are organised in clans. Their kings ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maibang">Maibang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaspur">Khaspur</a> until 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dimasa-people.jpg" alt="Dimasa people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khdibyajyoti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barman_Dimasa_Girl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Dimasa is explained as people of the big waters, referring to the Lohit, an upper tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> known in Dimasa as Dilao. Another name used within the community, Ha-Tseng-Sa or Ha-Cheng-Sa, means people of the soil, and may be the older one. Dimasa tradition holds that sixty thousand lunar months ago the people left their ancestral land in a severe drought and, after long wandering, settled where the Dilao meets the Brahmaputra. They belong to the Bodo-Kachari family of peoples, with the Boro, Tripuri, Rabha, Garo and Tiwa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Dimasa kingdom was one of the early states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> that followed the downfall of the Kamarupa kingdom. After political troubles in the 18th century the Dimasa ruler moved south into the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a>, and the people divided: the hill Dimasa kept their traditional way of life and political exclusiveness, while many in the plains converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> in the 19th century. These are known as Barmans and are recognised as a Scheduled Tribe under the name Barmans in Cachar, to distinguish them from the Barman Kacharis of western Assam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Dimasa society is sealed: each member takes a father's line from one of the male clans, called sengphong, holder of the sword, and a mother's line from one of the female clans, jalik or julu. The clans are spread over twelve territorial sacred groves, the daikhos, held to be the houses of the gods. Most Dimasa keep their animist and shamanic faith in spiritual beings known as madai; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a> it has blended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>.</p><p>The village headman, the Khunang, holds executive and judicial powers, assisted by the Dillik and the Daulathu. The Dimasa language is agglutinative, has six vowels and sixteen consonants, and is written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Eastern Nagari scripts; the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 137,184 native speakers. Since 1994, 27 January has been the Busu Dima festival day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dima-hasao">Dima Hasao</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dimasa people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Dimasa Kacharis</b> ruled the Dhansiri valley from Dimapur, whose carved stone pillars survive, by the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kachari-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
